Let[] in `std::vector` only checks bounds with `_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` or `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS`. me check what flags are being used:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-09-02T15:36:52.890Z","tool_name":"execute_command","tool_id":"tooluse_9eFKbMAdmZSf8vnkOHnDxg","parameters":{"command":"grep -r \"_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS\\|_GLIBCXX_DEBUG\" /home/khushiid/DiskANN/CMakeLists.txt 2>/dev/null"}}{"type":"tool_result","timestamp":"2026-09-02T15:36:52.891Z","tool_id":"tooluse_9eFKbMAdmZSf8vnkOHnDxg","status":"error","error":{"type":"tool_error","message":"Error from tool execute_command: Exit code: 1\n\nCommand failed with exit code 1: /bin/ksh -c 'grep -r \"_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS\\|_GLIBCXX_DEBUG\" /home/khushiid/DiskANN/CMakeLists.txt 2>/dev/null'"}}{"type":"tool_result","timestamp":"2026-09-02T15:36:57.448Z","tool_id":"tooluse_8G5y8uBFVBRMwymiZoQxxt","status":"success","output":"#define _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS 1\n#ifndef _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS\n#  define _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS 1\n#  define _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS 1\n#ifdef _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS"}{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-09-02T15:36:57.448Z","tool_name":"execute_command","tool_id":"tooluse_8G5y8uBFVBRMwymiZoQxxt","parameters":{"command":"cat /opt/rh/gcc-toolset-15/root/usr/include/c++/15/x86_64-redhat-linux/bits/c++config.h 2>/dev/null | grep -i \"_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS\\|SANITIZE\" | head -5\ncat /opt/rh/gcc-toolset-15/root/usr/include/c++/15/s390x-redhat-linux/bits/c++config.h 2>/dev/null | grep -i \"_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS\\|SANITIZE\" | head -5"}}`_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` is defined by default in the s390x c++config.h! That's why `operator[]` asserts on s390x — the assertions are baked into the header. On x86 they're also defined (the first line), but apparently not enabled by default there.

Let me verify:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-09-02T15:37:00.895Z","tool_name":"execute_command","tool_id":"tooluse_dYWQGgqbJ2YDicITlcdaUO","parameters":{"command":"grep -A2 \"_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS\" /opt/rh/gcc-toolset-15/root/usr/include/c++/15/s390x-redhat-linux/bits/c++config.h | head -10"}}{"type":"tool_result","timestamp":"2026-09-02T15:37:00.896Z","tool_id":"tooluse_dYWQGgqbJ2YDicITlcdaUO","status":"success","output":"#define _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS 1\n#endif\n\n--\n#ifndef _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS\n# if defined(_GLIBCXX_DEBUG)\n// Debug Mode implies checking assertions.\n#  define _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS 1\n# elif ! defined(__OPTIMIZE__) && ! defined(_GLIBCXX_NO_ASSERTIONS)\n// Enable assertions for unoptimized builds."}`_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS 1` is unconditionally defined in s390x's c++config.h! This means `std::vector::operator[]` always bounds-checks on s390x. This is a system-level difference — we can't change it.

The real bug is that `dist_scratch` is cleared (size=0) but then written into via `distances[i]` as if it were sized. The fix should be to `resize` rather than assuming the capacity is enough.

